-- UNT Math Millican Colloquium Presents: Dr. David Zureick-Brown
-- Source URL: https://calendar.unt.edu/event/unt-math-millican-colloquium-presents-dr-david-zureick-brown
-- Auto-created location: General Academic Building, 461 (33.213086, -97.148117)
-- Match reason: auto-created location from ICS coordinates
WITH
existing_import_location AS (
    SELECT location_id
    FROM locations
    WHERE lower(name) = lower('General Academic Building, 461')
    LIMIT 1
),
inserted_import_location AS (
    INSERT INTO locations (name, description, coordinates)
    SELECT
        'General Academic Building, 461',
        'Auto-created from UNT calendar event import.',
        ST_SetSRID(
            ST_MakePoint(-97.148117, 33.213086),
            4326
        )
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (
        SELECT 1 FROM existing_import_location
    )
    RETURNING location_id
),
resolved_location AS (
    SELECT location_id FROM existing_import_location
    UNION ALL
    SELECT location_id FROM inserted_import_location
    LIMIT 1
),
existing_event AS (
    SELECT e.event_id
    FROM events e
    JOIN event_details d ON d.event_id = e.event_id
    WHERE d.metadata ->> 'source_uid' = 'tag:localist.com,2008:EventInstance_52313330058596'
      AND COALESCE(d.metadata ->> 'source_recurrence_id', '') = ''
    LIMIT 1
),
updated_existing_event AS (
    UPDATE events
    SET
        title = 'UNT Math Millican Colloquium Presents: Dr. David Zureick-Brown',
        description = 'Beyond Fermat''s Last Theorem

 

In this talk, we''ll discuss numerous generalizations of FLT — for instance, for fixed integers a, b, c> 2 satisfying 1/a + 1/b + 1/c < 1, Darmon and Granville proved that the single generalized Fermat equation x^a + y^b = z^c has only finitely many coprime integer solutions. Conjecturally, something stronger is true: for a,b,c \geq 3, there are no non-trivial solutions. More generally, we''ll discuss Dr. Zureick-Brown''s subfield "arithmetic geometry", and in particular the geometric intuitions that underlie the conjecture framework of modern number theory.

 

David Zureick-Brown (DZB) is a professor of mathematics at Amherst College. He received his Ph.D. from the University of California, Berkeley, in 2010, where he was advised by Bjorn Poonen. After graduate school, David spent one year as an RTG postdoc at the University of Wisconsin-Madison and then joined Emory University as an assistant professor in 2011. After a little over a decade at Emory, David moved to Amherst College as a full professor in 2023. His research interests are broad, spanning number theory, arithmetic geometry, and algebraic geometry, with a particular focus on connecting subareas of mathematics. Throughout his career, David has received many grants and awards, including an NSF CAREER grant.
 

Millican lectures are made possible through the generosity of Mr. Olin Moore Millican (1904-1999), who established the Roy McLeod Millican Memorial Fund in honor of his brother.

 

The talks typically take place on Mondays from 4:00 to 5:00 p.m. in GAB 461. They are intended for the general mathematical audience (including undergraduates) at UNT. Everyone is encouraged to attend. Refreshments are served 30 minutes before the talk in the same room.

 

For more information, contact Dr. Lea Beneish.',
