updated_existing_event AS (
    UPDATE events
    SET
        title = 'UNT Math Millican Colloquium Presents: Dr. Tom Benhamou',
        description = 'On the Structure of an Ultrafilter Basis

 

In the first part of the talk, we will present background and motivation from topology, as well as Moore-Smith convergence of nets, for the study of the cofinality of ultrafilters. We will then discuss a problem posed by Kenneth Kunen in the 1970s concerning the existence of ultrafilters with small character on uncountable sets, followed by our recent contributions to this question (joint work with Moore and Serafin), as well as an answer to a related question of Dilip Raghavan and Saharon Shelah (joint work with Eskew). We will present some ideas from the proofs and related results. If time permits, we will present new optimal results regarding the cofinal structure of ultrafilters on the natural numbers.

 

Tom Benhamou is a Hill Assistant Professor of Mathematics at Rutgers University. His research focuses on logic and foundations, particularly set theory, ultrafilters, forcing theory, large cardinals, and connections to topology and infinite combinatorics. He earned his Ph.D. in Mathematics from Tel-Aviv University under the supervision of Prof. Moti Gitik, where he studied intermediate models of Prikry-type forcings. His work is supported by the National Science Foundation. Outside of mathematics, Tom plays the piano and is the father of two children, Lilly and Lev.

 

Millican lectures are made possible through the generosity of Mr. Olin Moore Millican (1904-1999), who established the Roy McLeod Millican Memorial Fund in honor of his brother.

 

The talks typically take place on Mondays from 4:00 to 5:00 p.m. in GAB 461. They are intended for the general mathematics audience (including undergraduates) at UNT. Everyone is encouraged to attend. Refreshments are served 30 minutes before the talk in the same room.

 

For more information, contact Dr. Lea Beneish.',
